Work out what it takes to show the network drive's queue

A design note, not an implementation. `docs/DESIGN-NOTES.md` gains a section on
showing what is in the eight-partition Samba drive described in `TENANCY.md`,
*"The hook is the product"* — the kiosk page that tells somebody whether their
file made it.

It is written up now because the design converged across two sessions with
station-node, and the reasoning is worth more than the conclusions. Nothing is
built and nothing here is a commitment.

## What it settles

**A browser cannot speak SMB**, so the shape is forced: something that can see
the drive walks it and publishes an index; the page renders the index. That is
the split `KIOSK.md` already describes, arrived at a second time from a
different direction.

**Completeness cannot be observed — it has to be declared.** The obvious design
is "same size across two scans means it landed," and it fails exactly when it
matters: a stalled writer is indistinguishable from a finished small file, so a
3 GB copy that died halfway gets reported as the best case. The index carries
three states, and the third is named `unwitnessed` because it names the absence
of a declaration rather than asserting a condition. Two traps that follow are
recorded with it — `arriving` → `unwitnessed` is signal loss that reads as
progress, and a file that finishes between two scans never passes through
`arriving` at all.

**Identity is declared too**, for the same reason. A FAT label is a name, not an
identity — nothing stops all eight partitions being called `MEDIA` — and Samba
serves a filesystem rather than a block device, so the volume serial underneath
is a coin toss. A minted marker file is the answer, with its trade written into
its own header: a marker is copyable where a serial is not, so **a duplicate id
means a copy was made, not that a volume moved.**

**The limits are part of naming the volume.** The 4 GiB / 2 GiB file ceilings,
and the FAT16 root directory that reports a full disk with gigabytes free
because long filenames eat several entries each. Masters do not fit on this
drive; it routes proxies, deliverables and sheets. These are filed upstream in
`tiliv/station-node#160` and repeated here because they constrain this artifact
directly.

**The one kiosk rule this artifact cannot keep** is `--check`. A queue index
describes a drive that changes every few seconds, so there is no build to attach
it to and no commit that could be current. Named rather than glossed, because
`--check` is what makes the kiosk's freshness a promise instead of a habit, and
the honest substitute is per-observation frozen instants so a renderer can show
staleness directly.

## Findings recorded so nobody repeats them

The router is at 10.209.1.1, a Linksys with SMB open on 445 and 139, and it
**rejects anonymous and guest sessions** — null, `guest`, `nobody` and `admin`
all refused. Enumeration is one command once a credential exists, so nobody has
to type out a list of partitions, but the first real listing waits on either a
saved keychain credential or anonymous access being turned on. That choice is a
network decision and it is left open.

## Scope

Also records Autumn's narrowing on sheets: the first job is the automatic
thumbnail pass off a Drive link, `proofing` stays out of it, and no human in the
loop means no approval state and a sheet that is derived rather than durable.

`docs/` is outside the Jekyll build root, so nothing here can reach a public
URL. `site/bin/test_no_secrets.py` passes.
